+/*
+ * The Fresco Logic FL1100 USB 3.0 host controller (1b73:1100): QEMU's
+ * xHCI given the part's registers and its one documented misbehaviour.
+ *
+ * From Fresco Logic's FL1100 datasheet (revision 1.10, August 2012):
+ *
+ *	configuration space	revision 00h; power management at 40h,
+ *				MSI at 50h (eight vectors, 64-bit, no
+ *				per-vector masking), PCI Express at 70h,
+ *				MSI-X at B0h with eight vectors, its
+ *				table in BAR 2 and pending bits in BAR 4
+ *	HCSPARAMS1 08000820h	eight ports (four USB 2, four USB 3),
+ *				eight interrupters, 32 slots
+ *	HCSPARAMS2 84000054h	16 scratchpad buffers with Scratchpad
+ *				Restore, ERST max 5, IST 4
+ *	HCSPARAMS3 00040001h	U2 exit under 4us, U1 under 1us
+ *	HCCPARAMS  200073A1h	64-bit, 32-byte contexts, no port power
+ *				control, no secondary stream IDs, streams
+ *	RTSOFF 2000h, DBOFF 3000h
+ *
+ * HCCPARAMS here keeps QEMU's extended capabilities pointer and does
+ * not claim light HC reset (LHRC), which QEMU's xHCI does not do.
+ *
+ * And from Linux (xhci-pci.c, "xhci: Enable trust tx length quirk for
+ * Fresco FL11 USB controller", 5.15, and "xhci: Fresco FL1100
+ * controller should not have BROKEN_MSI quirk set", 5.16): a short
+ * transfer completes with Success and the residual in the length, not
+ * Short Packet (XHCI_TRUST_TX_LENGTH) -- found streaming video, so on
+ * bulk, interrupt and isochronous transfers; not on control transfers,
+ * of which Linux would warn at every enumeration.  Its MSI and MSI-X
+ * work.
+ *
